# 13 — Mutability Markers

Mark classes, fields, variables, parameters, functions, and other symbols to show whether they are meant to change, using the features of the language version in use.

In TypeScript, use `const` unless the variable is reassigned, `readonly` for fields and properties set only once, and `readonly` array types for collections that are not modified. In Java, use `final`. In Kotlin, leave classes and members final unless they are meant to be extended, and only then mark them `open`.
